From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: BestROM <bestrom@localhost>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] addon.d: stop preserving /system/etc/hosts across an OTA

Nullroute L0 (SPEC §4 Phase 0, §8.1 step 5) ships /system/etc/hosts as a
build-time prebuilt_etc — `nullroute_etc_hosts`, which `overrides:` the stock
`etc_hosts` module from system/core/rootdir/Android.bp. It is generated by
packages/apps/Nullroute/tools/gen_l0_hosts.py and is part of the signed,
verity-protected image.

LineageOS's addon.d survival list still carries `etc/hosts`, from the era when
AdAway and its descendants wrote the user's blocklist into /system/etc/hosts at
runtime. With that entry in place, backuptool backs the file up before /system
is formatted and restores it afterwards — so the newly built hosts file is
overwritten by the previous build's copy on EVERY OTA. Consequences:

  * L0 is silently pinned at whatever shipped on the device's first flash. New
    entries never arrive, and the ~2,000-entry floor rots in place.
  * On a device that ever ran a hosts-file blocker, that tool's file is
    resurrected on top of ours — including whatever it had sinkholed to
    127.0.0.1, which is the loopback trap in SPEC §3.5.
  * The failure is invisible: /system/etc/hosts exists, is non-empty, and looks
    plausible. Nothing logs, and the hosts-probe.nullroute.invalid line from the
    older build still answers 127.0.0.8, so even the L0 liveness probe reports
    healthy. The only symptom is blocking that quietly stops improving.

Nullroute's mutable state is the NRDX index under /data/misc/nullroute, which
addon.d does not touch, so nothing needs to survive here. The list is left
empty rather than the functions removed: keeping list_files()/backup/restore
intact means the next path that genuinely needs preserving is a one-line change,
and ADDOND_VERSION stays at 2 so backuptool's compatibility check is unaffected.

Applying
--------
    git -C vendor/lineage am   packages/apps/Nullroute/rom/50-lineage.sh.patch
  or
    git -C vendor/lineage apply packages/apps/Nullroute/rom/50-lineage.sh.patch
  or, if the context has drifted after a vendor/lineage rebase, the whole change
  is "delete the etc/hosts line from list_files()" and this is equivalent:
    sed -i '/^etc\/hosts$/d' vendor/lineage/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh

vendor/lineage is an upstream repo, so `repo sync` will revert this. Keep it on
a topic branch with `git rerere` enabled, the same way §8.3 handles the
DnsResolver fork.

Verifying
---------
    grep -n '^etc/hosts$' vendor/lineage/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh  # no output
    sed -n '/^list_files/,/^}/p' vendor/lineage/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh
    # -> "list_files() {" / "cat <<EOF" / "EOF" / "}" and nothing between them
    grep -n '^etc/hosts$' $OUT/system/addon.d/50-lineage.sh                 # no output

Both `git am` and `git apply` were verified against the lineage-24.0 copy of
this file (43 lines, md5 221a6186b903550de87e9370d899c198).
---
 prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh | 18 +++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh b/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh
--- a/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh
+++ b/prebuilt/common/bin/50-lineage.sh
@@ -3,15 +3,27 @@
 # ADDOND_VERSION=2
 #
 # /system/addon.d/50-lineage.sh
-# During a LineageOS upgrade, this script backs up /system/etc/hosts,
-# /system is formatted and reinstalled, then the file is restored.
+# During a LineageOS upgrade /system is formatted and reinstalled; every
+# path listed in list_files() is backed up first and restored afterwards.
+#
+# etc/hosts used to be in that list because AdAway and friends write the
+# user's blocklist into /system/etc/hosts at runtime. Nullroute does not.
+# L0 is a build-time prebuilt_etc (nullroute_etc_hosts, which overrides the
+# stock etc_hosts module) and the mutable blocklist is an NRDX index under
+# /data/misc/nullroute, which addon.d never touches. Restoring etc/hosts
+# across an OTA would put the PREVIOUS build's file back over the freshly
+# installed one, pinning L0 forever at whatever the device first shipped
+# with -- and on a device that once ran a hosts-file blocker, resurrecting
+# that tool's file instead.
+#
+# The list is intentionally empty. Add a path back only if it is
+# user-writable state that lives on /system.
 #

 . /tmp/backuptool.functions

 list_files() {
 cat <<EOF
-etc/hosts
 EOF
 }

--
2.43.0
